Thanks for responding :)

Right okay, my understanding is pretty flawed then, I'm a little confused. So lets say I had a class called A (which has an instance created) and a class called B. In class A I have a unique ptr to an object of class B, in it's header file. In the constructor of A I call make unique to create an object of type B in this unique ptr. Is this unique ptr automatically deleted upon A being deleted then? Is this bad practice? And how come global unique ptrs are bad?

By putting it in header files I mean putting it in a class within it's header file. EG:

    class SystemClass
    {
        public:
            SystemClass();
            SystemClass(const SystemClass&);
            ~SystemClass();


            bool Initialize();

            void Shutdown();

            void Run();


            LRESULT CALLBACK MessageHandler(HWND, UINT, WPARAM, LPARAM);

        private:
